hi there steve kaufman here today and i  want to talk about frustrations in  language learning at different stages of  language learning and some of the things  that i do  to  work my way around these frustrations  um i am going to be first of all if you  enjoy these videos please subscribe uh  tomorrow i leave for montreal i'm gonna  spend a week there the city where i grow  up i grew up and i'm going to be taking  part in the long fest the montreal  language festival  uh we're gonna have the participants  from all over north america from all  over the world in fact  and i'm really looking forward  uh ollie richards who was interacting  with me here on my youtube channel  pointed out how difficult it is to learn  vocabulary in asian languages this is  true  i remember from japanese for example you  know at first and this is true of most  languages everything sounds the same  wakarimasu kawarimasu which is two  different things  but in time things become clearer but  there is ongoing frustration as we  continue to not understand forget and  the frustrations or the kinds of things  that frustrate us can be different at  different levels in our learning  now i'm now learning hebrew  i've kind of put greek on the back  burner but i want to get back to my  greek  and  so i'm at the very beginning stages i  have forgotten the frustrations that i  went through with japanese or even with  greek uh four or five months ago i'm  very much aware of the frustrations that  i have in hebrew it's difficult to read  the  vowel uh typically  vowels vowels are not indicated and even  in the when they do show the vowels they  have these microscopic little dots so  hard to see  and i don't really use them very much i  prefer on  link to rely on the voice the text to  speech that pronounces these words for  me but i'm continuing to find it  difficult and i've read a number of  stories many many times listen to them  many many times and continue not to  understand i do understand when i read  the story and i can look up the words  that i've forgotten  but then when i go to listen afterwards  the stories are all some stories 60 some  stories 20  so it is frustrating but i know that i  have to continue to expose myself to  this content because in time it'll  become clearer  uh because the writing system in hebrews  is not consistent in the sense that say  spanish is even in greek you have you  know a number of sounds that can be  represented by several  uh  combinations of of letters  but when you read it  i mean it is more consistent than hebrew  hebrew is is uh  it's it's very let me just let me just  say that it's worse than english so that  is a a level of frustration  but i know i have to continue to expose  my brain to this so  rather than and i have mentioned before  that if you do the same task over and  over again  uh comes a point when the the benefits  the return on investment of time  becomes less and less and less so you  have to vary either you have to vary the  content you're dealing with or you have  to vary the activity so  um i  want to stay with these i have 30 mini  stories in hebrew i want to stay with  these stories i want to try to get to  where i can understand these stories but  by listening to them over and over again  the brain is now saying stop tilt i've  heard them very often i don't understand  them leave me alone so what i do and  what i started doing more and more now  is using tagging  as a mechanical activity i don't like  uh quizzes you know match the hebrew  word with the english word i don't i  don't want to do that i just that's just  the way i'm  i'm made i don't want to do that i i  would rather passively expose myself to  the content but in different ways so i  tagged i began by tagging  whether you know for verb adjective  adverb preposition  because it wasn't obvious to me which  was a verb and which was a preposition  and which was a noun and so forth i  don't tag nouns because there's so many  nouns  so i tagged  those and then i was able to review  those in our what we call activities at  link now at length there is the flash  card there was the multiple choice  there's a fill in the blank closed test  there's dictation and there's what we  call a reverse flash card where you have  the word in english and you have to come  up with the meaning in the target  language well i just use the two easiest  in other words flash cards and multiple  choice  because first of all when the flash card  pops up you hear the text-to-speech  which i like  and i don't spend a lot of time trying  to remember it but most the time i can  remember i know what it means when i see  it when i hear it so that is another way  of reviewing these words even though  when i later on hear them in the context  of a story i forget them  but by tagging them as verbs now i know  that what i'm looking at here you know  when i review them i review 10 or 15  verbs or 10 or 15 adjectives  and that has helped a little bit to sort  of call it deconstruct the language  then i've now refined my approach  somewhat and i tag  and not just for verbs wherever i have  tagged verb i go in and i tag i have  created tags for  think so anything that has to do with a  thought  i can remember different  verbs of thought or might be different  forms of the verb saying thought i call  that think anything that has to do with  i intend to i'm going to i plan to  that's called plan  uh feel is another tag i use for afraid  bored i can't remember them all but  pretty soon  uh i collect  or for food or anything having to do  with learn  because there's you know teach learn  class school  this kind of stuff so i collect  things and i'm experimenting and i think  it's important to experiment and find  out which tags are most useful but i  find it very helpful to go through 10 or  15  of these items vocabulary items uh in  these two activities which is the flash  card where i hear it text to speech and  then again the multiple choice where  again i hear a text-to-speech so i'm  being exposed to it but the advantage is  that it's a mechanical  learning activity i go through the  lesson that i read three four five ten  times and i now go through and i  determine  you know what is the role or function of  this word and i give it  a tag that in itself is already helpful  and it's somewhat mechanical and then i  go to our activities section in the  vocabulary section that link and i go  through 15 or 20 of these  uh  motion for example is one  you know go come leave enter any of  those words that's under motion  uh anything having to do give take by  that sort of action of grabbing taking  giving that's another i call that take  you can give it your own definition and  some of these tags work better than  others and you can change as you go  along  uh  but it gives you another way of exposing  yourself  and and sort of focusing on a certain  type of word  and i find that when i go back and read  and listen i understand better now as  you progress in the language you'll find  different things challenging uh i should  say first of all that having spent now  two weeks on hebrew when i go back to  greek i find greek very easy  so one advantage of pushing yourself and  something more difficult is when you go  back to something that you're more  familiar with it all of a sudden seems a  lot easier than it was before  plus i've listened to the greek stories  many many more times  and as i say the writing system is not  as strange to me as the hebrew writing  system  but in slavic languages of course i tag  for  cases  in other words this is the instrumental  case or the genitive case or i tag for  you know verbs emotion aspect of verbs  these kinds of grammatical structural  things that cause me trouble and in  slavic languages i tag phrases because  i'm more advanced  so  i may eventually want to  with the slavic languages  also instead of just doing the simple  flashcards in multiple choice i might do  the reverse flashcard where i have to  translate from the english into  polish ukrainian or whatever it might be  so by varying what it is you choose to  save and tag word or phrase  sentence even  and which activities you're going to use  you can continue to vary this sort of  call it mechanical activity so it is  another way that you can advance your  learning  doing something that's  somewhat mechanical as a relief from  continuing to  uh struggle against you know  particularly in the early stages this  fact that you keep forgetting your feet  you keep not understanding  uh i find that in the languages where  i'm more advanced  i don't really need to tag for  vocabulary because i understand it so  well  that i know with time that things are  going to fall into place the words are  going to start to stick but that there  are certain structural patterns that my  brain is just not yet  used to and so i tend to focus in on  those areas it might be subjunctive in  spanish or whatever it might be so just  a bit of advice that you can use this  tool of tagging and experiment with it  as a means of overcoming the kinds of  frustrations that we inevitably come  across in language learning so i hope  this was  helpful  i look forward to seeing any of you that  are going to be in montreal  in this coming week  and thank you for listening bye for now  [Music]  you
 

